  "query_preview": "..The axis of the trough that has been bringing \nprecipitation to northern Utah is currently just to the west of \nKSLC. This is bringing the coldest 700 mb temperatures (between -9\nand -10C) over northern Utah with westerly flow aloft. This has \nkept showery activity on-and-off through much of the evening. This\nactivity will continue, especially southeast and east of the \nGreat Salt Lake which will provide some lake induced instability \nto initiate more showers overnight. These showers will be \nrelatively brief, but as temperatures have cooled to near freezing\nacross many locations a quick dusting of snow or graupel is \npossible across area roadways. Some of the hi-res guidance does \nwant to develop a more organize band around sunrise tomorrow \nmorning somewhere between Salt Lake City and Davis county. If this\ndoes develop then an inch or two of additional snow can't be \nruled out.\n\nHigh pressure redevelops over the area tomorrow which will help \nto bring drier conditions to the region, however temperatures will\nremain cooler than normal by about 5 degrees. This ridge will be \nshort-lived as another lower amplitude trough moves into the Great\nBasin on Monday. 53% of ensemble members keep conditions \nrelatively dry with this trough, while the other 47% of members \nbring light to moderate precipitation. With 700mb temperatures of\n-9C with this upcoming trough there should be enough cold air to \n support snow down to valley floors. Given that over 50% of \n members keep conditions relatively dry there is very little QPF \n in the 50th percentile (a few hundredths across the northern \n terrain), however there are some wetter members. The 90th \n percentile has 0.25-0.5\" of QPF across northern Utah with a \n favored area across Weber and Davis counties as a result of the \n westerly flow expected with the incoming trough.\n\nConditions will get quiet again for Tuesday/Wednesday as a \ntransient ridge develops. Guidance for the second half of next \nweek is a bit more uncertain. ~50% of the members remain dry while\nthe other 50% bring in moisture from an atmospheric river making \nlandfall in the PNW. This moisture is ushered into northern Utah \nalongside northwesterly flow. This could bring appreciable \nmoisture to the mountains of northern Utah if this pans out, so it\nwill have to be something to watch to see which solution guidance\ntrends towards.",
